  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x<0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}?$",
  "worked_solution": "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|3x+6|=-(3x+6).$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|3x+6|}{x-4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x<0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3x+6)}{x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{6}{x})}{(\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-(3+\\frac{6}{x})}{(1-\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(3+0)}{1-0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-3.$",
